The paper’s first decisive fact is already clear: it studies the above-average decision problem, not “recover the optimum,” and proves FPT/kernelization in parameter \(k\). That means any family with fixed or small \(k\) is disqualified as easy; if a Track A family exists, \(k\) must grow and the generated distribution—not merely the paper’s worst case—must survive the standard ordering-CSP attack. I’m now reading the reductions and kernel bounds in full and checking the harness implementation.
